## Native labels and translations

English labels are here so you can find the entry. Keep the translation Home Assistant shows, and the names people supplied. Explain an unfamiliar term near the task.

The underscore `_` in the native-label tables is a default translation entry. It is not an entity ID. Do not paste it into configuration.

## Source attribution

The official Home Assistant website identifies its source under [CC BY-NC-SA 4.0](https://github.com/home-assistant/home-assistant.io/blob/644bcefb1b42d1f50d26847d7d11df06c3b84ae2/LICENSE.md). This register preserves its term names, aliases and source links; it links to the official definitions instead of bundling them. The household explanations and examples were written for civica.

The extracted native keys and English labels come from Home Assistant Core and frontend contributors under [Apache License 2.0](https://github.com/home-assistant/frontend/blob/e32bc0696ebefde323f5b791269ad0c57f3f99d9/LICENSE.md). The [register's provenance record](../reference/ha-terminology.json) includes the licence text, exact sources and changes made to their organisation. Those native labels retain their upstream licence; they are not presented as original civica translations or relabelled as MIT material.

## Maintain an entry

1. Decide whether the text is a native label, a name someone supplied, a technical value or your own explanation.
2. Read the official source for the supported version.
3. Keep the native name and any literal value. Write a separate short explanation and the most likely mix-up.
4. Record the entry ID, source, date and version. Keep old keys that saved configuration still uses.
5. Check the example in a normal, a missing and an unclear state.

The [terminology JSON](../reference/ha-terminology.json) holds the source record. [Publish, update or retire content](publish-update-retire/index.html) explains evidence and change notes.
